              "type": "text",
              "content": "Quadrilaterals"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.853,
            0.071,
            0.88,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "67"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.11,
            0.883,
            0.21
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We started this section by examining the triangle formed by midpoints of the sides of a triangle. What happens if we use midpoints of the sides of a quadrilateral to make another quadrilateral? Experiment and see what this new quadrilateral looks like. It appears to be a parallelogram! Let us try to justify this using reasoning."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.238,
            0.451,
            0.392
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.145,
                0.238,
                0.451,
                0.392
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_16_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.253,
                0.401,
                0.349,
                0.42
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 12.21"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.477,
            0.225,
            0.882,
            0.42
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Geometric reasoning.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Suppose the midpoints of sides AB, BC, CD and DA of a quadrilateral ABCD are P, Q, R and S respectively. The Midpoint Theorem applied to ∆ABC and to ∆ADC gives, respectively, PQ ‖AC and SR ‖AC. So PQ ‖SR. Using the Midpoint Theorem in ∆BCD and in ∆BAD gives QR ‖PS. Therefore PQRS is a parallelogram."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.444,
            0.881,
            0.485
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We have deduced the following interesting fact, also known as Varignon’s theorem."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.489,
            0.881,
            0.549
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Theorem 9 (Midpoint Theorem for Quadrilaterals):",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The midpoints of the four sides of a quadrilateral are the vertices of a parallelogram. This is called the Varignon parallelogram of the original quadrilateral."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.367,
            0.59,
            0.63,
            0.618
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exercise Set 12.3"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.638,
            0.882,
            0.697
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. (i) If P, Q, R are the midpoints of sides AB, AC, BC respectively of ∆ABC, show that ∆PQR is congruent to ∆QPA and to two other triangles which you should identify."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.189,
            0.697,
            0.881,
            0.737
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) Suppose someone erases "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\Delta \\mathrm { A B C } _ { i }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , leaving only ∆PQR on the paper. Can you reconstruct "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\Delta \\mathrm { A B C }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " from "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\dot { \\Delta \\mathrm { P Q R 2 } }"
            }
          ]
        },
